  "body": "This removes the last use of old Web IDL \"read only arrays\" (i.e. the DOMString[] syntax), thus closing #11.\n\n---\n\nI went with the model of saying \"whenever things change\" instead of explicitly inserting an update step into the processing model, since it would be quite invasive. There's no central concept of updating the drag data store item list I could hook into (instead the many many methods that do so just use words like \"remove the item\" and \"add an item\"), and I didn't want to work on that kind of refactoring as part of this.\n- Tests: https://github.com/w3c/web-platform-tests/pull/3871\n- Gecko bug: https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1298243\n- Blink bug: https://bugs.chromium.org/p/chromium/issues/detail?id=652815\n- WebKit bug: https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=162932\n- Edge bug: https://developer.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/platform/issues/9191046/\n\nThis is a bit of a special case in terms of compat since nobody matches the spec as written and neither of the two existing implemented models is reasonable. (At the time they were implemented, FrozenArray was not a thing, and people did not recognize the importance of maintaining `x.y === x.y`.) I think we should move forward with this path; it's at the very least strictly better than just having invalid Web IDL syntax in the spec.\n\n/cc @bzbarsky; thanks for the analysis in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11#issuecomment-249155408.\n",
